    "suggested_action": "replace_ja_expression",
    "reason": "While both are nouns, the Japanese word '不可' (fuka) primarily means 'impossible,' 'not allowed,' 'unacceptable,' or 'failing grade.' It does not naturally convey the meaning of the English noun 'wrong' as in 'an injustice,' 'a mistake,' or 'an immoral act.' The JMdict gloss 'wrong' for '不可' likely refers to the adjectival sense of 'wrong' (incorrect, bad) rather than the noun sense of 'a wrong deed.' A learner would not expect '不可' for the noun 'wrong' in this context.",
    "suggested_ja_replacement": {
      "surface": "過ち",
      "reading": "あやまち",
      "reason": "'過ち' (ayamachi) is a common and natural Japanese noun meaning 'mistake,' 'error,' 'fault,' or 'wrong,' which aligns well with the English noun 'wrong' (an act that is wrong or an injustice)."
    }
